# TypeScript & Next.js Standards

## TypeScript

- `strict` remains on. No `any` unless justified with a one-line comment.
- Prefer `type` for props/unions; `interface` only when extension is intentional.
- Export named functions/components; default exports only for Next.js `page.tsx` / `layout.tsx` / `error.tsx` / `not-found.tsx` / `loading.tsx` / route handlers if convention requires.
- Colocate types with the module that owns them; share via `lib/` only when reused by two+ modules.

## Next.js App Router

- Default to Server Components. Add `"use client"` at the top boundary only.
- Fetch and transform data in Server Components or `lib/` loaders — never `useEffect` for static MDX.
- Use the Metadata API (`generateMetadata` / `metadata`) on every route that ships.
- Dynamic routes need `generateStaticParams()` for MDX-backed pages.
- Route handlers live under `app/api/**/route.ts`; only contact (+ OG) are expected.

## Imports & modules

- Path alias: `@/*` → project root.
- Prefer absolute imports for cross-folder references; relative only within a tight folder.
- Keep side-effect-free modules in `lib/` pure and unit-testable.

## Error handling

- User-facing UI never dumps stack traces.
- API routes: validate input, return typed JSON + correct status codes, never leak secrets.
